1
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,880
Crime linkage is the practice of

2
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,200
identifying which different offenses

3
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,600
were committed by the same offender.

4
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,640
For example, if you have a whole lot of

5
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:23,800
unsolved burglaries, you want to find

6
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,000
out which offenses are attributable to

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,760
the same person.

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,120
That is because you might have some

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,720
information about the perpetrator from

10
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,440
one crime, for example, a shoe print

11
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,160
that points to a male with a certain

12
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,520
shoe size and weight.

13
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:39,400
See if you could identify which other

14
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,120
offenses you think are linked to that

15
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,960
same person.

16
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,920
Perhaps some of those have other

17
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,160
information about the burglar, such as a

18
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:49,160
witness seeing that he has a tattoo on

19
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:51,600
one hand. And in this way, you can put

20
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:53,200
together more and more pieces of the

21
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,920
puzzle.

22
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,520
You now have good reasons to believe

23
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,200
that the offender who is responsible for

24
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,640
this particular series is of a certain

25
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,520
gender, weight, has a certain shoe size,

26
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:05,080
which might point to his approximate

27
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:08,360
height, and he has a tattoo on one hand.

28
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,120
If you allocate one of those pieces to

29
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:12,240
the wrong puzzle, i.e., to the wrong

30
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,920
series of offenses, you unnecessarily

31
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,800
hinder the investigation. So, it's

32
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,640
important to link the right offenses to

33
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:20,880
the right person.

34
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:22,200
Crimes can be linked through the

35
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,360
perpetrator's MO, or modus operandi,

36
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:29,160
which is how he carries out his crimes.

37
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:31,160
This can refer to the kind of targets he

38
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:32,800
selects and what he does to those

39
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,200
targets.

40
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,120
For example, Jack the Ripper's MO was to

41
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:38,800
attack prostitutes in London's

42
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,160
Whitechapel area in the East End during

43
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:44,200
darkness and to cut their insides.

44
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,960
If another prostitute was poisoned to

45
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,640
death in a different part of London

46
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,440
during the day, that would not be

47
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:51,560
consistent with Jack's MO, and we might

48
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:53,240
therefore regard that as being the crime

49
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:55,440
of another person.

50
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:57,480
People are creatures of habit, and if we

51
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:58,960
find a way of doing something that

52
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,040
works, we tend to stick with that way of

53
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,240
doing it as we have learned that it is

54
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:04,800
effective.

55
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,680
A skilled burglar who knows how to pick

56
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:09,320
locks is unlikely to kick the door down.

57
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:10,920
He is much more likely to continue

58
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:12,800
picking locks as that gets him through

59
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,800
doors quietly. So we're looking for

60
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,120
commonalities in decisions taken by the

61
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:19,840
offender, how and whom he targets and

62
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,200
how and whom he attacks for example. We

63
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:23,760
also need to take into account

64
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:25,040
progression.

65
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:26,800
A prolific offender might gain at least

66
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:29,000
experience but perhaps also additional

67
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,520
skills during the commission of each

68
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:31,760
crime.

69
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,560
When he makes a mistake on one occasion

70
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,920
for example that nearly got him caught,

71
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:37,680
he might take care to avoid that or

72
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,120
prepare for it next time round.

73
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:42,000
As we're looking for both consistency

74
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
across time but also progression, it is

75
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,160
important to identify in which order the

76
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:49,160
offenses took place.

77
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:50,720
Criminals also tend to become

78
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:52,760
increasingly confident as they continue

79
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,120
in their offending without getting

80
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:56,320
caught. So they might be willing to take

81
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:58,160
more and more risks.

82
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,040
Of course, the most reliable factor to

83
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,560
use when linking crime is forensics so

84
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,440
that when you find an offender's DNA at

85
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,280
every burgled property, you can safely

86
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:08,360
assume he has been there.

87
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,760
MO is another way of linking crimes but

88
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:12,800
it is not as reliable as geography which

89
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,360
I will introduce next.

90
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,360
Thank you for watching. I hope you found

91
